{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1494022-the-true-fae-the-ancient-rules-tricking-mortals-into-arcadia","id":1494022,"title":"The True Fae, The ancient rules & Tricking mortals into Arcadia","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-09-29T22:06:24-07:00","id":1494022,"title":"The True Fae, The ancient rules & Tricking mortals into Arcadia","content":"<hr /> <div align=\"center\"><i><b>Is there a rule that says that the True Fae cannot just kidnapp someone to their realm? To what degree can an average mortal deal with them in the material?</b></i></div> <hr /><br /> <span style=\"font-family:lucida sans unicode\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><i>Many Folk Tales require the Fae to get permission, trick or beat the mortal at a game in order to be able to keep them there.<br /> <br /> We know that the realms of the Fae are bound by the ancient rule of sacred hospitaly, they can create dangers in the realm but the realm itself must be something the average human could survive and changes must happen gradually, following a pattern. Could it be that the rules of sacred hospitality also binds the other physical forms of the Fae?<br /> <br /> I imagine some kind of rule like If you invited them, as a consecuence you have to behave like a good host, however they also have to behave like a good guest: Eating something without permision, Attacking the Fae, Stealing from it, Winning a bet etc... Seems like the kind of thing that allows the Fae to exert their powers on the transgresors. I am thinking of stuff like the greek myths, poems like the goblin market or one of my favourite movies &quot;Coraline&quot;.<br /> <br /> The Fae always seems to be tricky across the media. They also seem way weaker in the material world, like if reality was breathing poison and it weakned them, after all, if they where god like in the material they would not behave in this way. Neither would fear so much cold iron as even if it could damage them, they could just outspeed or overpower the mortal be it with magic or raw strenght. By observating their behaviour, they seem to go after children because they are less physically capable of retaliation, easier to trick, and more emotional wich means more food.</i></span></span><br /> <hr />I posted it because I would like to hear other people though on the true fae limitations after all how can you have a good story without adversity and conflict.<br /> <br /> PD: Also how do you create spoiler brackets? I wish I could use some examples but I don\u00b4t want to spoil certain amazing movie.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.deviantart.com/cicerondixit/gallery\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.deviantart.com/cicerondixit/gallery</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24037-leandro16","name":"Leandro16","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24037_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"552"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-29T23:41:58-07:00","id":1494024,"title":"","content":"The main reason the Fae generally play subtle is because they're predators and/or greedy enough to want to minimize their losses. The important thing to remember about the Others is that they set up the rules of engagement to favor themselves whenever possible, and sometimes that means playing silly buggers with the clever monkeys so they trip into your debt-slavery racket instead of waiting for a random vagrant to wander off of the main roads and grabbing him.<br /> <br /> <br /> Spoilers can be created by enclosing your text in the following brackets, minus the asterisks:<br /> [*spoiler]text[*